1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,759
I'm Alex Filareal with the VOA Special English Health Report.

2
00:00:06,759 --> 00:00:14,359
The crisis at the damaged Fukushima Dai Iti nuclear power station in northern Japan

3
00:00:14,359 --> 00:00:19,000
has raised worries about radiation risks.

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:25,679
We spoke with Jonathan Lynx, an expert in radiation health sciences.

5
00:00:25,679 --> 00:00:35,439
He is a professor at the Johns Hopkins Bloomberg School of Public Health in Baltimore, Maryland.

6
00:00:35,439 --> 00:00:43,799
Professor Lynx says workers within the nuclear plant are the only people at risk of extremely

7
00:00:43,799 --> 00:00:47,359
high doses of radiation.

8
00:00:47,359 --> 00:00:53,480
For other people, he says, there may be a long-term worry.

9
00:00:53,480 --> 00:01:00,480
People can get cancer from low doses of ionizing radiation, the kind released in a nuclear

10
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:02,320
accident.

11
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:10,960
Professor Lynx says scientists can use computers to quickly model where radioactive material

12
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:13,719
has blown and settled.

13
00:01:13,719 --> 00:01:19,400
Then, they measure how large an area is contaminated.

14
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:26,440
He says if the situation is serious enough, officials could take steps like telling

15
00:01:26,440 --> 00:01:33,000
people not to eat locally grown food or drink the water.

16
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:40,800
But he said that would only be the case if there was a major release of radiation.

17
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:48,760
And if it was shown that the radioactive material was blown over the area and then settled

18
00:01:48,760 --> 00:01:55,800
into and onto water, plants, fruits, and vegetables.

19
00:01:55,800 --> 00:02:03,620
The reactors at Fukushima are on the Pacific coast, but Professor Lynx says people should

20
00:02:03,620 --> 00:02:10,879
not worry about any radioactive material leaking into the ocean.

21
00:02:10,879 --> 00:02:16,840
He said even in the worst accident, the sea dilutes the radiation.

22
00:02:17,560 --> 00:02:25,159
So the amount of radioactivity in the sea water would still be quite low.

23
00:02:25,159 --> 00:02:31,680
Japan is the only country to have had atomic bombs dropped on it.

24
00:02:31,680 --> 00:02:39,599
That memory from World War II would create a stronger psychological sensitivity to radiation

25
00:02:39,599 --> 00:02:43,960
exposure Professor Lynx says.

26
00:02:43,960 --> 00:02:53,000
The 25th anniversary of the explosion and fire that destroyed a reactor at Chernobyl in Ukraine

27
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:55,560
was in April.

28
00:02:55,560 --> 00:03:04,080
The 1986 event was the world's worst accident in the nuclear power industry.

29
00:03:04,080 --> 00:03:14,360
A new United Nations report says more than 6,000 cases of thyroid cancer have been found.

30
00:03:14,360 --> 00:03:23,720
These are in people who were children in affected areas of Belarus, Russia, and Ukraine.

31
00:03:23,720 --> 00:03:32,760
The report says that by 2005 the cancers had resulted in 15 deaths.

32
00:03:32,759 --> 00:03:38,679
The cancers were largely caused by drinking contaminated milk.

33
00:03:38,679 --> 00:03:46,840
The milk came from cows that ate grass where radioactive material had fallen.

34
00:03:46,840 --> 00:03:51,359
For VOA Special English, I'm Alex Villareal.

35
00:03:51,359 --> 00:03:57,439
To get the latest updates, go to VOAspecialEnglish.com

